#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143674} :activity/id #uuid "d3c63d19-b2be-5958-b93c-b6adbeba66d1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050115 :topic/id #uuid "2e4072ab-aef1-5dac-919a-4fa75cc98c63" :topic/math-academy-id 2380} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143674 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144085 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to multiply a fraction by a mixed number. For example,\n\n$$\n1 \\,\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times \\dfrac{4}{7}\n$$\n\nTo solve this problem, we can use the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Convert the mixed number to an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1 \\,\\dfrac{1}{3}=\\dfrac{(1 \\times 3) + 1}{3}= \\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Multiply the fractions by multiplying the numerators and denominators separately:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{3} \\times \\dfrac{4}{7} = \\dfrac{4 \\times 4}{3 \\times 7} = \\dfrac{16}{21}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Simplify or convert back to a mixed number, if necessary. In this case, no simplification is needed because the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{16}{21}\n$$\n\nis a proper fraction.\n\nSo, the answer is\n\n$$\n1 \\,\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times \\dfrac{4}{7} = \\dfrac{16}{21}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "738a78c1-dc39-5272-835f-a143e50534e8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8630 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2170dd51-6df3-55a4-b9b7-00f7896da663" :step/math-academy-id 135774 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143675}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143675 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142801 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142622 :question/id #uuid "294b83f4-2e38-5bf4-a521-01fe8ca13fe5" :question/math-academy-id "e-13831"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ef8389c-2909-5e02-a185-817ae3c6219b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050634}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143303}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Numerator When the Result Is a Proper Fraction"} :step/id #uuid "6765eeb